EXT. RIVER OF ASH - FERRY LANDING - DUSK

Gray water. No current, no wind. A rickety dock lit by a single lantern. Beyond it, a flat ferry with a rope-pulled mast.

THE FERRYWOMAN, 60s, in a cloak the color of dust, stands at the stern with a long pole. She doesn't speak. She counts the figures lined up on the dock: shadows with hats, hands, the vague outline of people.

A VOICE, deep, ancient, from the lantern, as if the flame were a mouth.

LANTERN VOICE (O.S.)
How many tonight?

She holds up four fingers, then one more.

LANTERN VOICE (O.S.) (CONT'D)
Five. That's all?

She nods. Raises her pole. Plants it in the water.

She turns to the shadows.

FERRYWOMAN
(to the whole line)
Step aboard. One at a time. No pushing.

She waits. They shuffle forward. One shadow stops at the rail.

She leans toward it.

FERRYWOMAN (CONT'D)
(to that shadow)
You owe me a coin.

The shadow doesn't move. She sighs. Pulls a coin from her cloak and drops it in the water.

FERRYWOMAN (CONT'D)
(muttering)
Third time this month. Nobody carries their own coin anymore.

LANTERN VOICE (O.S.)
I heard that.

FERRYWOMAN
You were meant to.

She pushes off. The ferry drifts. The lantern recedes behind them. Gradually, quietly, it dims, and the voice goes with it.

LANTERN VOICE (O.S.) (CONT'D)
(faint)
Don't be late.

FERRYWOMAN
(to the dark, flat)
I am never late.

A spark flies off the pole. For a heartbeat she is lit from below, and her face is young. Then it is gone.

FERRYWOMAN (CONT'D)
(to the shadows, softly)
Hold on. It gets colder.
